Excellent follow-up 👏 — this is the most important concept behind how StatefulSets manage storage per pod.

Let’s break it down in very simple terms, like how it works in real-world Kubernetes clusters (e.g., AWS EKS) 👇

🧩 Recap: What We Want to Achieve
Each pod in a StatefulSet — like mongo-0, mongo-1, mongo-2 —
should have its own independent disk (volume) so that:

If a pod restarts → its data remains safe

If a new pod is created → it gets its own clean storage

If pod mongo-0 is deleted → its volume is not reused by mongo-1

🧱 How Kubernetes Manages Volumes per Pod in a StatefulSet
Step 1️⃣ — You define volumeClaimTemplates in the YAML
volumeClaimTemplates:
- metadata:
    name: mongo-data
  spec:
    accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
    resources:
      requests:
        storage: 5Gi
This means:

“For each pod in this StatefulSet, automatically create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) of 5 GiB named mongo-data.”

Step 2️⃣ — Kubernetes automatically creates one PVC per Pod
When StatefulSet runs with replicas: 3, it does this:

Pod Name	PVC Name	PV (Persistent Volume)
mongo-0	mongo-data-mongo-0	EBS volume (5 GiB)
mongo-1	mongo-data-mongo-1	EBS volume (5 GiB)
mongo-2	mongo-data-mongo-2	EBS volume (5 GiB)
✅ Each PVC is unique to that pod.
✅ PVCs are bound to PersistentVolumes (PV), which are usually AWS EBS disks in EKS.

Step 3️⃣ — Each Pod Mounts Its Own PVC
In the container section:

volumeMounts:
- name: mongo-data
  mountPath: /data/db
When mongo-0 starts:

It mounts mongo-data-mongo-0 → /data/db

When mongo-1 starts:

It mounts mongo-data-mongo-1 → /data/db

So each pod sees /data/db — but backed by its own EBS volume.

Step 4️⃣ — What Happens When Pods Restart?
Let’s say mongo-1 crashes.
Kubernetes does:

Recreates mongo-1 (same pod name)

Reuses the same PVC mongo-data-mongo-1

Pod mounts the same EBS volume → data is still there

✅ Data persists
✅ Pod identity (mongo-1) remains stable
✅ Perfect for databases, queues, etc.

Step 5️⃣ — What If You Scale Up or Down?
Scale up to 4 pods:

New pod mongo-3 is created

New PVC mongo-data-mongo-3 is automatically created

New EBS volume provisioned

Scale down to 2 pods:

Pod mongo-2 is terminated

PVC mongo-data-mongo-2 remains (data safe)

You can later scale back up → it will reuse the same PVC

💡 Real-Time Example in AWS EKS
If you deploy this StatefulSet on EKS with default StorageClass (e.g., gp2 or gp3),
Kubernetes automatically provisions EBS volumes as PersistentVolumes.

You can check them via:

kubectl get pvc
Output:

NAME                 STATUS   VOLUME                                     CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE
mongo-data-mongo-0   Bound    pvc-12345abc                              5Gi        RWO            gp3            5m
mongo-data-mongo-1   Bound    pvc-67890def                              5Gi        RWO            gp3            5m
mongo-data-mongo-2   Bound    pvc-11223xyz                              5Gi        RWO            gp3            5m
Then you can verify in AWS console → EC2 → EBS volumes — you’ll see three volumes created.

🧠 Analogy (to Understand Simply)
Think of a StatefulSet like a small apartment building:

Tenant (Pod)	Room (Volume)	Label
mongo-0	Room #0	“mongo-data-mongo-0”
mongo-1	Room #1	“mongo-data-mongo-1”
mongo-2	Room #2	“mongo-data-mongo-2”
Even if tenant leaves and comes back (pod restarts),
he gets the same room (volume) back — with his belongings intact!

🧾 In Short
Concept	Explanation
volumeClaimTemplates	Template to auto-create PVC per pod
PVC name pattern	<template-name>-<statefulset-name>-<ordinal>
Persistent Storage	Each pod gets a unique disk (PV)
Restart behavior	Pod reuses same PVC (data persists)
Scale behavior	New PVCs created only for new pods
